    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<h2>Why claim documentation matters</h2>
<p>A property insurance claim is built on evidence. The insurer needs to understand what was damaged, when and how it happened, and what it will cost to fix. Good documentation makes that easier to show and harder to dispute. It also helps if questions come up later about the date of loss, pre-existing wear, or items missing from the estimate.</p>
<p>The challenge is timing. Evidence fades quickly in the Coastal Bend: hail marks get rained on, water dries, debris is hauled away, and emergency repairs cover what was there. This claim documentation checklist helps Corpus Christi property owners capture the important details while they are still visible.</p>

<h2>Photo and video tips</h2>
<ul>
<li><strong>Start before cleanup.</strong> Photograph damage before moving anything, then again after emergency mitigation.</li>
<li><strong>Go wide, then close.</strong> Take a wide shot that shows where you are, a medium shot of the area, and close-ups of specific damage.</li>
<li><strong>Show scale.</strong> Place a coin, ruler, or tape measure next to hailstones, dents, or cracks.</li>
<li><strong>Record all sides.</strong> Walk around the entire building and capture each side, including fences, outbuildings, docks, and HVAC units.</li>
<li><strong>Mark water heights.</strong> After a hurricane, photograph high-water lines with a tape measure. The difference between wind-driven rain and surge often decides which policy responds.</li>
<li><strong>Use video for context.</strong> A slow walkthrough with narration (date, location, what you are seeing) ties individual photos together.</li>
<li><strong>Keep the originals.</strong> Do not edit or crop the original files; the embedded date and time can be useful.</li>
<li><strong>Stay safe.</strong> Do not climb onto a damaged or wet roof. Photograph from the ground or let a professional inspect it.</li>
</ul>
<p>For hail, see <a href="/document-hail-damage-for-an-insurance-claim/">how to document hail damage for an insurance claim</a>. Business owners should read <a href="/document-commercial-property-damage-after-storm/">how to document commercial property damage after a storm</a>.</p>

<h2>Organizing your claim file</h2>
<p>Keep everything in one place, either a folder or a cloud folder you can reach from your phone. Many coastal owners have more than one policy, so keep a separate section for each insurer:</p>
<ol>
<li><strong>Policies:</strong> the full homeowners or commercial policy, the TWIA windstorm policy, and any flood policy, with declarations pages and endorsements.</li>
<li><strong>Claim basics:</strong> claim numbers, date of loss, adjuster names and contact information for each insurer.</li>
<li><strong>Photos and video:</strong> organized by date and area of the property.</li>
<li><strong>Weather evidence:</strong> storm reports for your date of loss. The <a href="/storm-lookup/">storm lookup tool</a> finds NOAA and National Weather Service reports near your city or ZIP code.</li>
<li><strong>Estimates and invoices:</strong> contractor estimates, the insurer's estimate, mitigation invoices, permits, and receipts.</li>
<li><strong>Windstorm paperwork:</strong> any WPI-8 certificate of compliance for the building or past roof work.</li>
<li><strong>Contents list:</strong> damaged personal property or business contents with descriptions, ages, and costs where known.</li>
<li><strong>Correspondence:</strong> letters, emails, and your communication log.</li>
</ol>

<h2>Keeping a communication log</h2>
<p>Every conversation about your claim is part of the record. For each call, email, or visit, write down the date and time, who you spoke with, their role, what was discussed, and any next steps or deadlines. Follow up important calls with a short email summarizing what was said. A clear log helps you track requests and deadlines; the <a href="/claim-deadline-calculator/">claim deadline calculator</a> shows the Texas timelines that may apply.</p>

<h2>Your privacy</h2>
<p>The checklist runs entirely in your browser. Your checkmarks and notes are not sent to us or anyone else. If you choose "Save my progress on this device," they are stored only in this browser's local storage, so they will not appear on another device and will be lost if you clear your browser data. Use the print or download buttons to keep a copy.</p>
